1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:05,000
This video shows how to install UM-Codex on a Windows computer.

2
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:07,080
First, what to have ready.

3
00:00:07,080 --> 00:00:10,520
Then, the one command that installs it.

4
00:00:10,520 --> 00:00:15,160
Then, Docker Desktop, the administrator step, and your key.

5
00:00:15,160 --> 00:00:17,240
And last, how to open UM-Codex.

6
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:22,600
UM-Codex runs Codex in a sealed environment on your own computer.

7
00:00:22,600 --> 00:00:27,780
You need a Windows computer, your key for the University of Michigan's GPT Toolkit,

8
00:00:27,780 --> 00:00:30,580
which is in Aman's email, shared on Dropbox,

9
00:00:30,580 --> 00:00:34,080
and Docker Desktop, which runs the sealed environment.

10
00:00:34,080 --> 00:00:38,192
If Docker Desktop isn't installed, setup installs it for you.

11
00:00:38,692 --> 00:00:42,852
Installing Docker Desktop needs administrator permission, just once.

12
00:00:42,852 --> 00:00:47,672
On a Michigan Medicine computer, you request temporary administrator access first.

13
00:00:47,672 --> 00:00:51,412
It's turned on from your profile page, under administrator access.

14
00:00:51,412 --> 00:00:53,332
Give it at least thirty minutes.

15
00:00:54,532 --> 00:00:58,172
Open the Start menu, type PowerShell, and press Enter.

16
00:00:58,172 --> 00:01:01,332
Open it the ordinary way, not as administrator.

17
00:01:01,332 --> 00:01:05,716
The installer asks for administrator permission itself, only if it needs it.

18
00:01:06,216 --> 00:01:11,136
Paste the install command from UM-Codex's page on GitHub, and press Enter.

19
00:01:11,136 --> 00:01:12,496
It's one command.

20
00:01:12,496 --> 00:01:16,248
It works through seven steps, and each one says what it's doing.

21
00:01:17,448 --> 00:01:19,348
Step one gets Windows ready.

22
00:01:19,348 --> 00:01:23,048
Docker Desktop needs a Windows feature called WSL.

23
00:01:23,048 --> 00:01:28,752
On this computer, WSL and Docker Desktop are already there, so it moves on.

24
00:01:29,252 --> 00:01:30,712
On a computer without them,

25
00:01:30,712 --> 00:01:34,432
step one lists what it needs to change, and asks if you're ready.

26
00:01:34,432 --> 00:01:39,212
Windows then shows a box asking to allow changes to your device.

27
00:01:39,212 --> 00:01:39,212
Click Yes.

28
00:01:39,212 --> 00:01:42,792
A second window does the work, which can take ten minutes or more.

29
00:01:42,792 --> 00:01:43,852
Leave it open.

30
00:01:43,852 --> 00:01:49,004
Windows then restarts, and the installer carries on by itself after you sign in.

31
00:01:49,504 --> 00:01:51,164
On the Michigan Medicine network,

32
00:01:51,164 --> 00:01:54,984
a Windows policy can stop Docker's virtual machine from starting.

33
00:01:54,984 --> 00:01:59,164
If that happens, the installer says so, and offers to fix it now.

34
00:01:59,164 --> 00:02:03,804
Windows asks for administrator permission once, and the right is given back.

35
00:02:03,804 --> 00:02:07,480
Or you can restart Windows, and run the installer again.

36
00:02:07,980 --> 00:02:10,180
Step two starts Docker Desktop.

37
00:02:10,180 --> 00:02:13,520
Steps three to five install UM-Codex itself,

38
00:02:13,520 --> 00:02:17,900
with its own copy of Python, and download the sealed environment's image.

39
00:02:17,900 --> 00:02:20,916
The download is the long part, the first time.

40
00:02:21,416 --> 00:02:23,516
Step six asks for your key.

41
00:02:23,516 --> 00:02:27,196
Copy it from Aman's email, paste it, and press Enter.

42
00:02:27,196 --> 00:02:32,216
Each character shows as a star, and the key is saved in Windows Credential Manager.

43
00:02:32,216 --> 00:02:36,608
It never goes into the sealed environment, and it isn't kept in any file.

44
00:02:37,108 --> 00:02:42,028
Step seven adds UM-Codex to your Start menu, and a shortcut to your Desktop.

45
00:02:42,028 --> 00:02:45,004
Then it's done, and it tells you how to open it.

46
00:02:46,204 --> 00:02:51,084
To open UM-Codex, double-click the UM-Codex icon on your Desktop.

47
00:02:51,084 --> 00:02:54,700
You can also open the Start menu, and type UM-Codex.

48
00:02:55,200 --> 00:02:57,940
It opens UM-Codex's window in your browser.

49
00:02:57,940 --> 00:03:01,400
That's a page running on your own computer, not a website.

50
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:05,020
The line at the top says Docker is running and your key is saved.

51
00:03:05,020 --> 00:03:10,608
Under it, Get started lists what is left: make your first setup, which is the next video.

52
00:03:11,108 --> 00:03:13,128
That's UM-Codex installed.

53
00:03:13,128 --> 00:03:18,020
In the next video, Your first launch on Windows, you start Codex in a folder.
